WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.250
- Hello, I'm Ray Dalio,
and I'm happy to be here

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.220
with Paul Volcker, who
is my greatest hero.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.250
And, probably to many people,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.120
the greatest living American,
and that's a big statement.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.430
I've watched Paul evolve since 1970,

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.550
when he was Under
Secretary of the Treasury

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.320
and Monetary Affairs, all
the way through the present.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.610
And, during those years,
he had more affect

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.410
on the world economy, and
built up more credibility,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.600
and has been at the center of
more economic decision making

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.050
than any man alive.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.050
In his 91 years, he's affected
the world in profound ways,

00:00:45.422 --> 00:00:47.900
some of which are very famous,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.340
and some of which are just a
reflection of his credibility.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:56.340
For example, when the issue,
the controversial issue,

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.610
of the monies that Jews
lost in the holocaust

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.340
went into Swiss banks, and
that very delicate subject,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.530
very different from regular economics,

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.400
the man that they chose
to examine that question

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.060
was Paul Volcker.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.490
Paul, in many, many different occasions,

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.940
and at many different times,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.760
shaped many, many different issues.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.760
So, this is a man who has
extraordinary principles.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:29.970
I've known him to be the most
principled person that I know,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.950
and also to have been at the center

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.970
of most important economic decision making

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:40.840
during those many, many
years, for many decades,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.220
and to see things from the top.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.950
This is a man with great perspective,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.950
and, so, I'm here today to pull
out the principles from Paul

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.610
and to understand how
he got those principles.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.610
So, Paul, to start off, please
give me what you consider

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.670
to be what your most
important principles are.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.530
- Well, first of all, thank you

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.520
for those very generous comments,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.740
and let me say I'm glad to
see you here at this point.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.700
I've been a little under the
weather, I hope I'm recovering.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.720
But, you know, it's a book I just wrote,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.570
we got something to talk about.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.700
You ask about my principles
and the first thing

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.350
that comes to mind, which
I'm sure we'll get to later,

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.120
is my first principle
is effective governance.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.040
And I go back and we can
discuss this in more detail.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.190
Alexander Hamilton put
it all in a nutshell.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.230
Alexander Hamilton is an old treasury guy,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.920
he's a financial guy,
he restored the credit

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.420
of the United States,
so he's a hero of mine.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.837
He said, "The true test of good government

00:02:48.837 --> 00:02:51.580
"is it's ability to administer."

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.460
Not the great policy but can
they carry out the policies

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.813
effectively and economically?

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.200
And good government is
not just high policy,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.970
it's making the machine work,
day after day, efficiently.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.000
Faith of the American people
in our government today

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.593
is really distressing.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.493
There have been polls taken every year,

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.640
ask the same question,
see how the trend changes.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.020
Do you trust your government
to do the right thing

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.810
most of the time?

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.253
Doesn't sound, the right
thing most of the time,

00:03:24.253 --> 00:03:27.570
Doesn't sound like all
that difficult a test.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:30.003
You get maybe 20% that say yes.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.930
You ask about the Congress,
it'll be less that 20%.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.240
And, it's no great secret,
that we're torn apart

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.430
by ideological and other differences now.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:42.800
So, we got a real challenge.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.390
So when you ask my principles,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.240
my first principle is good governance.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.070
When you talk about principles,
they go down in the family,

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.930
and we were not a family
that spent money easily.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.630
We liked to economize and be sure

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.400
how we were spending money.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.340
I'd like to think we carry
this over, or I carry it over,

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.090
when I had some
responsibilities in government,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.230
to try to do it sufficiently.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.320
Not only as accurately, but
as efficiently as possible.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.980
You know, my father, he took over

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:18.730
as city manager of this
bankrupt town in 1930,

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.580
and lasted for 20 years.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.070
But he became the principle
figure in the town,

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.850
and, no question, he was the best known

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:28.710
and, fortunately, highly respected.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:32.590
But he was a fanatic for disclosure.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.980
He would do a very
detailed budget every year

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.480
and distribute it to everybody in town.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.860
And it went down to how many
police cars they bought,

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.150
how many fire engines they bought,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.390
and he professionalized
the police department

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.293
and the fire department.

00:04:47.809 --> 00:04:51.870
Two departments that are
essential, obviously, in any town.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:56.870
And he was a bug for not spending
more than he had to spend,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.923
but spend what was necessary
for a professional operation.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.630
- It appears that he had a
profound impact on your life.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:10.470
You were born in 1927, this was two years

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.390
before the stock market crash
and the Great Depression.

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:17.480
In 1930, at the beginning
of the Great Depression,

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.620
he took you and the family
to Teaneck, New Jersey.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.510
By the way, read about this in this book

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.747
because it's the building of a great man

00:05:25.747 --> 00:05:28.730
and a system, to build a great system.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.830
And he came as the manager of that town.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.260
I'm gonna read a quote of your father's

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.187
that relates to what you're saying.

00:05:38.187 --> 00:05:42.837
"Government is a science, and
I'm happy that the officials

00:05:42.837 --> 00:05:44.987
"and the people of this community,

00:05:44.987 --> 00:05:47.527
"agree that the man who is the manager

00:05:47.527 --> 00:05:52.122
"should be thoroughly trained
in the science of government."

00:05:52.122 --> 00:05:55.046
And in that part of the book,

00:05:55.071 --> 00:05:58.880
you also refer to

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.229
that it was a solidly, middle-class town

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:06.580
and you refer to his good education,

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.750
his basic, good, solid education.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.640
And in the book you talk about
your good, solid education.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:18.140
You talk about the importance of family

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.180
and the science of government,

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.920
the idea of that good management,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:27.740
and you refer to its outcomes.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.820
That there was no crime
in Teaneck, New Jersey.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:35.310
The Saturday Evening Post wrote an article

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:38.740
that's entitled, There is No
Crime in Teaneck, New Jersey.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.830
Because it was well managed,
because there's a middle-class,

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.970
because there's good education.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.160
I'm interested in what
you think is happening

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.960
to infrastructure, education,

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.960
family, middle-class, and
government management.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:01.940
And you expressed worries about
the United States' position

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.210
in the world.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.980
Do you think these things are related?

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:05.813
What are your thoughts on this?

00:07:05.813 --> 00:07:08.880
- Yeah, and all these things
you say are very familiar.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.570
But I grew up, precisely at the time,

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:14.100
when I graduated from
high school or college,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.000
World War II was over, the
United States had won the war,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:17.993
in our opinion.

00:07:19.082 --> 00:07:23.630
It rules the world, it
developed, presumably,

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.704
a multicultural, multilateral

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:30.345
system of governance

00:07:30.345 --> 00:07:34.310
and mutual growth.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.730
It all seemed very successful,

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:38.990
we kind of forget that we
had two miserable wars,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:41.570
in Vietnam and then in Iraq.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:43.760
But, by in large, I grew up in a world

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.430
where I moved into the
United States Treasury,

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.960
I felt the kingpin of
treasuries around the world

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:50.880
because--
- You were!

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.040
- Well, I wasn't, the US Treasury was.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.230
- Yes, the US Treasury was.

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.370
- And the Federal Reserve.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.610
It's different now,
we're learning in a world

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:02.080
where we're still top dog, maybe,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.010
but top dog isn't so readily
recognized by others as it was.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.651
And we obviously had the rise of China

00:08:09.651 --> 00:08:12.542
as a clear competitor

00:08:12.542 --> 00:08:14.010
for world influence.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.520
And how that all works out, we'll see,

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:21.520
but it's not the comfortable
world of assumed leadership

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.420
that I grew up with.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.940
And that's what I struggled
with in the Treasury

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.070
and, later, in the Federal Reserve.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:33.620
There are other forces, the
rest of the world is growing,

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.940
some of them more rapidly than we.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:38.900
When I was in the Federal Reserve,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:42.750
Japan was the big question as to the rival

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.900
of the United States,
and growing very rapidly.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.133
Has very good success economically.

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.720
We kind of forget about
that now, as things change,

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:53.673
now it's China.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.188
But we have to learn in a world

00:08:58.188 --> 00:09:00.013
in which leadership

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:03.580
by America is not taken
for granted anymore.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.360
And I'm afraid that's been speeded up

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.440
by what's been happening in
Washington, and the country,

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.990
in the last four or five years.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.587
- How so?

00:09:12.587 --> 00:09:15.290
- I think we, obviously,
got these dividers,

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:19.460
divisiveness in the Congress,
divisiveness in the country.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.030
It's made it impossible
to take very coherent,

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:27.350
consistent approaches to
our domestic problems,

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.930
but particularly toward foreign policy.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.971
And that sense, again,

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:36.280
that we are the natural leader,

00:09:36.288 --> 00:09:38.853
we have certain allies
that seemed permanent

00:09:39.958 --> 00:09:43.240
10, 15 years ago that don't
seem so permanent anymore.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:45.950
It's the toughest situation
that we have to accommodate to

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:49.230
that makes some emphasis
on effective government

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.940
all the more important, in my mind.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:55.290
Who is going to appreciate
the United States as a leader

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.310
if we don't seem to be able to manage

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:58.850
our own affairs at home?

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:02.850
- You keep referring to
management of the government,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:06.070
and your father was the manager,

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:10.610
and you've been around the
management of all governments

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:11.753
around the world.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:17.960
You've also explained
that the discouragement

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:22.960
of government workers to
be professional managers.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:24.660
Could you embellish on that point?

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.820
I know this is, maybe,
probably, your greatest mission,

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.100
is to have a beneficial impact on that.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.208
- Yes, you know there are

00:10:33.908 --> 00:10:38.849
two million federal employees,

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:42.110
Not counting the Army, the
Air Force, and so forth.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:43.863
Two million civilian employees,

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.550
which is exactly the same amount we had

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:50.633
back in the Kennedy administration,
which is 80 years ago.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:55.290
And you ask how is the
government operating,

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:56.533
same number of people?

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.993
It does an awful lot of
outsourcing, out-contracting.

00:11:03.903 --> 00:11:06.410
The defense department, itself, outsources

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.850
300 billion a year, or more.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.200
Who's minding the store?

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.650
And I'm not sure we're
well enough equipped

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.530
for those two million people.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.260
I headed a commission on the civil service

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:23.410
when I left the Federal
Reserve, which was in 1987,

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:24.790
and there was a big commission,

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:29.040
we had ex Vice Presidents,
we had ex cabinet officers,

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.630
we Congressmen, we had a lot of people.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.230
We issued a good report.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:34.970
What impact did it have, very little.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.343
About how you needed to develop a

00:11:37.343 --> 00:11:40.040
more effective civil service.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:43.250
Make it easier to hire,
make it easier to fire,

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.690
get the right leadership in there,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.163
which some departments
have and some do not have.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.710
Five years later, I
headed another commission.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.230
Much smaller commission
with another good report.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:57.913
But what impact did it have?

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.640
Not enough.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.350
One of the observations
that's often struck me

00:12:05.241 --> 00:12:07.790
is there's a feeling by many business men,

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:10.530
the government's not efficient,
doesn't do things right,

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:13.810
shouldered a lot of time,
service, and not very helpful,

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:16.660
which, obviously, there
are isolated places

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.370
where all those things are true.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.740
But, by enlarge, I have
been struck by the number

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.550
of businessmen who have
been called into government

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:27.740
for secretary or something
or other, under secretary,

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.450
in the White House or wherever,

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:31.370
who, after two or three
years in government

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.410
say I didn't realize
there were so many people

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.810
in the government that
knew what they were doing,

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.280
and can do it effectively.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:43.440
And it's one of the biggest
experiences I take away

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.150
from my years in government.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.730
That it's much more
effective, potentially,

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.210
with a better staffing than I thought.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:54.200
But that's a hard lesson
to sell, generally.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.570
And it's not always true.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.250
We have to improve the damn thing

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:01.720
and that's what I'm after.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.970
- At this point in your life,

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.300
the thing that you have
devoted the most to

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:12.300
is the proper,
professionalization, education

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.530
of government managers.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.150
You've created the Volcker Alliance,

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:23.460
in which you've devoted
most of your net worth to,

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.210
in order to foster the proper development

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:32.010
of government workers in that way.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.030
You've spoken to me in the past

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:37.130
about how they're not respected,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:41.260
and, also, how it's
difficult at universities,

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:45.890
and various places, to get
them to be attracted in there

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:48.320
because they're not
given a good career path,

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.320
they're not given good training,

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:53.490
and they're not given much respect.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:57.460
And that that is undermining
the effectiveness

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.410
of the system.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:00.740
What are your thoughts about that?

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:04.220
- Thank you, you preached
my sermon very well.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:07.110
I have been concerned about the strength

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:08.183
of the civil service.

00:14:09.569 --> 00:14:11.530
People who want to go in the civil service

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:14.790
often can be selected, maybe in a school

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.340
or public administration,
say, okay, you got a job,

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:20.360
it's February, but we can't
have you, we can't pay you

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.580
until the new fiscal year,
which is six months away.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:26.470
So, these people can't
sit around with no pay

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.150
or anything for six months,

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:31.580
so they go get a job on Wall
Street, or some place, instead.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.760
And that's typical of the kind of thing

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.690
that needs to be repaired.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.690
But what concerned me is the attitudes

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:41.020
toward government itself
and the difficulties

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:44.440
in getting and attracting
and keeping the best people.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:49.170
Paralleled by the growing
lack of interest, as I see it,

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.000
in universities.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.000
50 years ago, 100 years ago,
schools had departments,

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.590
so schools of public administration.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.000
Public administration's a bad word now.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.160
People associate public administration

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:05.280
with a bunch of time servers,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:09.760
sitting in some government
office, producing nothing useful.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.650
Administration, people don't think that

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.677
when you say business administration,

00:15:14.677 --> 00:15:18.680
but they think it when you
say government administration.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:23.320
But we have a problem in these schools.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:27.080
Many of them have lost
sight of how to teach.

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.740
What are they teaching, what
should the curriculum be?

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:33.860
How do we train people to
oversee all that outsourcing?

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:36.880
Hundreds of billions of
dollars being outsourced.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:38.220
Who's minding the store?

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:40.050
Are people equipped to do that?

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.140
Have we taught them the right thing?

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:43.640
Have we taught them how to manage?

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.230
Have we taught them how to debate issues

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.010
as a right of good memorandum?

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:50.683
How to deal with other
people, other departments.

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:55.910
And, so, this little Volcker Alliance

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.940
is trying to get a kind of
marriage, a new marriage,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.773
between universities and the government.

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:06.740
State and local government as
well as federal government,

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:10.050
to see whether we can
develop a stronger sense

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:13.303
of interest and efficiency.

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.460
Last time this was true
in the United States

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:18.970
was before World War I,

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:22.767
when there was a great movement
for improving government.

00:16:22.767 --> 00:16:25.630
And the civil service came in to effect.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:28.523
And up to the Depression years,

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.960
there was a lot of pressure, a lot of done

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:34.913
to improve government organization.

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:38.440
And a lot of people wanted
to go in the government.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:43.070
Now we are, what, 80 years
later, we've lost much of that.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.150
- So, would you say a principle of yours

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.520
is that in order to have
effective government

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:52.740
you have to have effective civil servants.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.390
- Whether you call them civil servants,

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:55.904
or whatever you call them,

00:16:55.904 --> 00:16:57.400
you have to have people running it.

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:01.350
I'm with Alexander Hamilton,
the true test to government

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:04.350
is the ability to get
something done efficiently.

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:08.830
And we lapse in that respect.

00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:13.830
- You've been in contact with
economics in every country,

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:15.970
every major country.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:18.180
Is that a distinguishing characteristic

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:21.720
of those that are successful
and those that are not?

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.520
- You have to have a great
feeling of commitment

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.790
to whatever you're doing,
whether it's economists

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.660
or civil servant.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:32.380
They still exist, there's
still a lot of people

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:34.330
in government who have a
great sense of commitment

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:38.400
to doing what's going on and, you know.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.500
Look at this present change in government.

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:46.490
I think there's something
like 700, maybe more,

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:50.050
potential presidential
appointments in the new government.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.430
All the secretaries and
the secretaries assistants,

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:56.490
secretaries judges, so forth and so on.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:58.813
About a third of those
offices have failed.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.960
Now, nobody's paying attention.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.530
Now, who's running those departments

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:07.633
while the civil servants are left there,

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:12.740
maybe in good form, maybe not,
but leaderless, basically.

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:14.633
That isn't the way to run a government.

00:18:15.539 --> 00:18:18.370
And that's just one
example of the difficulties

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.090
we're having these days.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:24.420
- Looking around the world,
what are the principles

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:27.419
of great government?

00:18:27.419 --> 00:18:30.120
- Well, obviously, you need
a strong leader at the top,

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.080
that helps, quite a lot.

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:34.996
But what we are running into

00:18:34.996 --> 00:18:38.662
is a life of democracies.

00:18:38.662 --> 00:18:41.130
Are democracies really able

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.480
to do the kind of thing I'm talking about,

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:45.640
or do they eventually, as Plato said,

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:48.600
end up with some plutocracy?

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:52.300
And you can't really maintain a democracy

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:55.250
over a period of years.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:59.650
But you have countries
like France and Germany,

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:04.020
had a very disciplined, very
prestigious civil service.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:08.600
So did the UK, that's
deteriorated a lot in recent years

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.140
because it's considered too elitist,

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:16.140
too far removed from the public aspect.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.820
And I guess this a great shake.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:22.690
How can you get the
expertise that you need,

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:24.770
and the leadership that you need,

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:29.770
and still respect the popular desire?

00:19:31.094 --> 00:19:33.210
And the vote, in the end, the politicians

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.523
are going to decide the
direction in which to go.

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:40.033
- You referred to Plato's cycle,

00:19:40.408 --> 00:19:41.991
in the republic,

00:19:41.991 --> 00:19:45.403
the idea, of course, that
democracies are threatened.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:51.410
Describe that and describe how you feel

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.760
that that pertains to today's situations.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:56.790
- Well, I think if you
look at the United States,

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:58.910
that we're very familiar with,

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:01.043
you have a very divisive

00:20:02.959 --> 00:20:04.584
new administration.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:10.890
You have the kind of gridlock that we saw

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:12.600
in the Obama administration.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:15.160
I was friendly with Mr. Obama,

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.795
I was disappointed he
couldn't get more done,

00:20:17.795 --> 00:20:21.380
but here you got a situation
where the Congress,

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:23.330
in effect, said, the Senate said,

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:26.610
whatever Mr. Obama is for, we're against.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:31.490
And they maintained that
position for three years, really.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.540
And did the government
suffer from that? Yes.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:36.470
Did it have a really well designed,

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:40.236
efficient healthcare system

00:20:42.603 --> 00:20:44.119
for the uninsured?

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:46.560
They tried very hard but
it was very difficult

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:50.440
to develop it and maintain it.

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:54.023
And you see symptoms of that sort

00:20:54.023 --> 00:20:56.789
all over the place now.

00:20:56.847 --> 00:21:00.410
An inability to reach
some reasonable consensus

00:21:00.410 --> 00:21:03.150
on how to administer certain programs,

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.150
or how, in the first
place, to get agreement

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:06.750
on what the program ought to be.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:11.010
And it's not a phenomenon
just for the United States,

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:11.903
unfortunately.

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:16.823
Obviously, in Eastern
Europe, Latin Americas,

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:22.363
the hardship case, almost,
from top to bottom.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:28.200
But even within the established
democratic countries

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.175
in the euro zone,

00:21:33.091 --> 00:21:34.357
we're all having some trouble

00:21:34.357 --> 00:21:38.125
in maintaining the progress

00:21:38.125 --> 00:21:40.858
that we made internationally.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.680
And developing a set
of international rules

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:46.783
and developments that suit everybody,

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:51.100
and the feeling that they
don't suit everybody,

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:53.300
and that too many people
have been left out.

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:58.920
I think that's a real problem

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.642
that Mr. Trump has touched upon

00:22:01.642 --> 00:22:04.490
and kind of the real base for his support.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:07.810
But whether we're handling
in the right way is,

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.003
in my mind, pretty doubtful at the moment.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.570
- You had, probably, the
greatest economic challenge,

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:17.810
and you were both also the
greatest, most powerful man

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:21.210
in the country, in the world actually,

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:23.570
in terms of your control
over monetary policy.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:28.561
So, 14.8% inflation is where we hit

00:22:28.561 --> 00:22:30.819
in March of 1980,

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:34.630
and as a result of that, you
tightened monetary policy,

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:36.000
I remember it very well.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.200
You set an M1 target of 5.5%.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:41.350
And, as a result of that,

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:45.842
interest rates went up to 20%.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.873
- More than we ever expected.

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:52.960
- And when you tighten
monetary policy like that,

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:57.840
it drove the unemployment rate up to 10%

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.145
and it caused

00:23:01.145 --> 00:23:05.170
the worst downturn in that period of time

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:06.480
since the Great Depression.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.640
And you held tight in that.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.650
There were protests, there was anger,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:16.430
and you held tight in that
and you followed it through.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:20.660
And as a result of that, you
broke the back of inflation,

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:24.470
and in 1983, that inflation rate went down

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:27.770
to 3% inflation, at that time.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:32.160
And we began a decade of prosperity,

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:35.460
of strong growth with low inflation.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:39.880
Now, you had to have a
vision of what it was like,

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:42.120
you had to fight through that vision,

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.400
despite all the opposition.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:48.083
That's an example of leadership under,

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.320
people now look back and they say

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:54.010
that was a great accomplishment.

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:57.010
But, at that time, that must
have been very difficult.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:59.330
Can you describe it and then, also,

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:03.510
tell us more about what you've
seen to be the qualities

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:07.480
of great leadership that
you've seen in others as well?

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:10.290
- Well, this all happened,
the fight on inflation,

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:14.390
inflation itself, 50 years ago, roughly.

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:16.465
And people, today,

00:24:17.857 --> 00:24:20.781
people my age, are all dying off,

00:24:20.781 --> 00:24:25.050
and even middle-aged
people with children then.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:27.100
Now, you may not remember the atmosphere

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:30.670
as it was experienced, the
inflation rate had gone up.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:33.470
For more than a decade, there
were kind of feeble efforts

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:34.770
to deal with it.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.020
During the Ford administration,
they handed out buttons.

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:43.020
Whip Inflation Now, but it
had more buttons than policy.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:47.280
And there was always this
conflict, it's always existed,

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.480
don't tighten monetary policy too far,

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:51.360
you'll get some unemployment.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.280
So, we went a decade that
way and we got more inflation

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:57.360
and more unemployment.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:01.060
And, when I took over the Federal Reserve,

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:02.900
at the end of Carter's administration,

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:05.960
President Carter was totally frustrated

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.680
because he seemed to be unable to take

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.770
any initiative anyway,

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:12.360
because all this inflation
was backfiring on him,

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.200
he couldn't do anything in the budget,

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:16.120
couldn't do anything on energy,

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.350
all these things he
would've liked to have,

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:21.727
seemed to be stalled by
this fear of inflation,

00:25:21.727 --> 00:25:23.903
it was very real at that time.

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:26.280
And, so, he asked me to become

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:27.590
Chairman of the Federal Reserve.

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:32.540
Well, it was obvious that
existing policies weren't working.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:34.750
And, actually, I was in the
Federal Reserve before that,

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:37.240
voting against some of those policies.

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:39.280
But I decided we had to change in approach

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:44.280
and we've got to stop worrying
about increasing unemployment

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:47.430
at any time, we've got to
deal with this inflation

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:49.280
or it's going to get worse and worse.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:50.770
'Cause it was accelerating when it

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:53.540
got close to 15% annual rate.

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:57.810
And if we pussy-footed much
longer, we're going to be 20%.

00:25:57.810 --> 00:25:59.570
And people were really fearful

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.940
about the stability of the country.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:05.090
So, we did tighten, it took
a long while, actually,

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:08.190
for the unemployment rate to go up.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.350
It was very stubborn
and the inflation rate

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:15.350
was very stubborn, and we
made one or two false moves

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:17.640
that we had to take back,
it took a little longer

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:18.760
than we thought.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:21.650
But I saw no other way to approach this,

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:26.650
other than kind of a bulldog
biting at it, straight out.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.110
And I got plenty worried in '82

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:31.957
when we did begin getting unemployment,

00:26:31.957 --> 00:26:34.570
and the damn inflation
rate wasn't coming down,

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:38.610
and the money supply wasn't
coming down the way we wanted.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:42.330
But I felt that we were
stuck, we couldn't back off

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:47.620
or all the effort we were
making would be for naught.

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:51.903
Fortunately, by the summer of '82,

00:26:52.954 --> 00:26:56.810
the money supply came
down, inflation rate became

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:59.750
coming down, we had a recession.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:01.980
But by the end of the year,
it looked like the recession

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:04.790
could be over, or at
least stop getting worse.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:07.400
And it ended pretty quickly.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.408
But it took that last year

00:27:10.408 --> 00:27:13.358
of sticking with it

00:27:14.251 --> 00:27:16.860
that I think was necessary to do the job.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:20.040
Now, you talk about how
unpopular it was, yeah.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:22.640
The farmers had tractors
outside the Federal Reserve,

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:27.330
we had community groups,
we had one Congressman

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:31.050
spent every day, for two years,
asking for my impeachment.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:35.830
But my sense was the country
as a whole understood

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:38.893
that inflation was
getting us by the throat,

00:27:38.893 --> 00:27:42.585
and they were willing to sit back

00:27:42.585 --> 00:27:45.718
and not be too aggressive

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.560
because they thought I
was attacking a problem

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:50.430
that needed to be attacked.

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:54.860
And I had more support, I
think, well, I know I did.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:59.860
A good measure of that was
when my reappointment came on,

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:01.573
at the end of this process.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.460
I was first nominated, it was unanimous.

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:09.480
Nominated four years later,
it was not unanimous,

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:12.110
I had eight democrats voting against me,

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.620
eight republicans.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.010
They were eight left-wing democrats,

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:21.010
eight right-wing republicans,
and 84 votes with me.

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:26.163
And I think that was a fair
representation that people,

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:28.360
things were getting better then,

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:31.730
but people tolerated what it took

00:28:31.730 --> 00:28:33.850
to deal with the inflation problem.

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:36.200
And I don't think there was
any other answer, frankly.

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:39.410
I consoled myself, it was a tough time,

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:42.867
but do you have any other
answers, or what do you think?

00:28:42.867 --> 00:28:45.390
- No, I remember going through it.

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:48.740
Every single day, I was
wondering what Paul Volcker

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:50.590
was thinking since you said that.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:53.570
You set the M1 target of 5.5%,

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:56.350
and I did the calculations of velocity,

00:28:56.350 --> 00:28:59.820
and I thought, oh, my God, is that really

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.380
going to be happening?

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:04.330
Because you have a 14% inflation,

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:07.229
that means the interest
rates have gotta go up.

00:29:07.229 --> 00:29:08.960
So it was a time.

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:11.840
People don't have any understanding

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.870
of how challenging that time was.

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:18.870
We think of 2008, but the 1982,

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:22.440
the unemployment rate rising to 10%.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:25.470
The rate at which the economy plunged,

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:28.428
all of those conditions were worse

00:29:28.428 --> 00:29:31.211
than in 2008.

00:29:31.211 --> 00:29:34.970
So it was a more dramatic time,

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:37.810
even in terms of volatility
of interest rates.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:40.880
- But people forget about, going back then

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:42.640
as compared to 2008.

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:45.330
We had a bankrupt Latin America,

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:48.160
and the banks had head over heels

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:50.410
in lending to Latin America.

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:55.410
They had one and a half
to two times the capital,

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:57.010
not just big American banks,

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:59.370
but big international banks elsewhere,

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:02.090
invested in Latin America.

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:04.800
One day Mexico said, we can't pay anymore.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:06.638
- August, 1982, I remember it well.

00:30:06.638 --> 00:30:08.230
- August, 1982.

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:09.850
Then, what happened?

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:14.240
Well, we hook and by crook
and with the help of the IMF,

00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:18.980
which is very important, we
stalled, stalled for time,

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:22.320
and they go, oh, this
is not very dramatic.

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:25.950
We got the banks 'cause it
was in their own interest

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:30.490
to continue lending enough,
to pay interest anyway.

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:33.500
We forced constructed reforms,

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:37.860
we saw it on Mexico, Brazil, Argentina,

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:39.950
a lot of that's been lost since.

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:42.530
But that was with the help of the IMF.

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:47.362
And it held together, we
didn't have a banking collapse.

00:30:47.362 --> 00:30:48.800
It wasn't subprime mortgages,

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:52.330
it was subprime Latin American borrowing.

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:55.710
- It was a good example of how
you could roll things forward

00:30:55.710 --> 00:30:59.530
from 1982 until 1991
when they had the Brady--

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:00.670
- That is correct.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:03.240
Things as capital had been restored,

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.250
some of the debts had been lowered,

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.530
and he could finish it off

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.133
with the so-called Brady Plan, yes.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:14.240
- You have known most world leaders

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:16.856
for most of the

00:31:16.856 --> 00:31:20.206
last 50, 60, 70 years.

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:25.610
Tell me, what are your principles about

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:27.500
what makes a great world leader,

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:29.400
or a great leader in general?

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:31.080
- Well, sometimes when
you get close to them

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:32.330
they don't seem so great.

00:31:34.059 --> 00:31:37.553
When you look around at world
leaders in recent years,

00:31:38.781 --> 00:31:40.630
when you begin thinking
of, I think Mrs. Merkel

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:43.230
did a pretty good job
in Europe and Germany.

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:45.157
- We had dinner with
Lee Kuan Yew, you and I,

00:31:45.157 --> 00:31:46.110
you might remember.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:49.200
- Lee Kuan Yew was the next
name I was going to mention.

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:52.110
And the third name I
was going to mention is,

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.593
I don't know how much
you ran into Zhu Rongji.

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:58.480
- He's been a hero of mine,
I didn't know him personally.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.260
- Well, he was a hero of mine.

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:04.050
This was in the 1990s when
I was in China quite a lot,

00:32:04.050 --> 00:32:07.370
I don't go to China, very little now,

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:08.790
and I don't know the leaders now.

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:11.030
But I knew the leaders in the 90s,

00:32:11.030 --> 00:32:15.203
when they seemed to be a
blossoming, capitalist economy,

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:17.640
to exaggerate a bit.

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:21.090
With hopes for an open,
democratic society.

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:22.560
Doesn't look that way, quite, now,

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:27.560
but back when Dung and
Zhu Rongji were there,

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:30.610
it looked very promising.

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:32.560
And they'd come to us for advice,

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:35.320
they don't come to us for advice anymore.

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:38.510
They might come to us and tell
us what to do, but. (laughs)

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:40.445
- Well, they seem to
be doing pretty good--

00:32:40.445 --> 00:32:41.278
- Yeah, that's right.

00:32:41.278 --> 00:32:44.950
That's right, they say we don't
have to follow you anymore.

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:48.120
But Lee Kuan Yew, of
course, is a special case.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:48.953
Here he is--

00:32:48.953 --> 00:32:50.830
- But what are those qualities?

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:54.853
- Well, he knew what he wanted
to get done and he did it.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:59.030
And in a small, relative enclave

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:02.590
and he did a lot of things
that we tutted about,

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.407
in terms of democracy, he was

00:33:05.407 --> 00:33:07.727
a little too eager to put people in jail

00:33:07.743 --> 00:33:10.143
to quiet down opposition

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:13.220
and worry about what the
newspapers said about him

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:14.540
and so forth.

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:18.960
But he had a drive to develop Singapore

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:21.043
with a very strong civil service,

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:25.233
very effective, corrupt-free government,

00:33:25.233 --> 00:33:28.670
as near as I can see,
and he pulled it off,

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:31.660
and he maintained a very strong position.

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:35.820
And Singapore is,
economically, I don't know

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:39.250
where it ranks in the world by
pro-capita income, but it's--

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:40.083
- Way up there.

00:33:40.083 --> 00:33:41.150
- [Paul] Very high, come up from nothing.

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:44.100
- So, your principles,
if I get it correctly,

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:46.610
your principles would be a great leader

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:50.254
has an accurate vision of where to go.

00:33:50.254 --> 00:33:52.795
A great leader has,

00:33:52.811 --> 00:33:57.236
will fight the opposition

00:33:57.236 --> 00:33:59.440
in pursuit of that, and deliver.

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:02.670
A great leader has a deep
caring for the people

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:04.390
that they're leading.

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:07.860
A great leader also seems to be leading

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:12.085
with an effective management

00:34:12.201 --> 00:34:14.676
of civil service

00:34:14.676 --> 00:34:18.730
in government, or a effective bureaucracy

00:34:18.730 --> 00:34:20.587
to carry those things through.

00:34:20.587 --> 00:34:22.510
Are those the principles?

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:25.420
- Well, I think we have to add
a principle, the one you had,

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:28.290
you gotta have a respect for the people.

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:31.193
I don't know if you included that.

00:34:31.193 --> 00:34:32.970
Some of the qualities you mentioned,

00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:35.660
you could apply to Adolf Hitler.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:38.010
But we don't want an Adolf Hitler,

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:41.300
we want somebody who's got
some democratic instincts

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.900
and recognizes that he's
only there temporarily.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:49.793
And that he wants to lead
the country effectively,

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:53.316
but peaceably,

00:34:53.333 --> 00:34:58.332
and without dictatorial demands.

00:34:59.581 --> 00:35:01.900
So, it's a nice balance to be in charge,

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:06.350
and maybe less efficient
when you have to deal with

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:09.400
public opinion and
different points of view.

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:13.950
But it's obviously essential
if democracies going to last.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.780
We legitimately worry about democracy now.

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:19.690
My mother, who was quite
a character herself,

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:24.080
I recall, vaguely,
whining to her when I was,

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:26.190
probably, a young man in government,

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:29.337
Vietnam War or something,
rioting in the streets...

00:35:32.289 --> 00:35:34.870
We were threatened, the
country was threatened

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:37.810
to survive all these tensions.

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:42.370
She said, sonny boy, she didn't
call me sonny boy, (laughs)

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:46.310
she said we're 200 years of democracy,

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:47.710
we've been a longer democracy

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:49.400
than anybody else in the world,

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:51.780
we've gotten over worse than this before,

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:54.290
go get back to work and do something.

00:35:54.290 --> 00:35:57.330
And I take a little
comfort in that thought,

00:35:57.330 --> 00:35:59.590
that we can straighten ourselves out

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:03.473
after this little turmoil,
not so little turmoil.

00:36:05.740 --> 00:36:09.480
- What do you see the
United State's future,

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:14.100
and China's future, over the
next 10 years, let's say.

00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:17.240
- Well, you know, I
sometimes, it sounds terrible,

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:20.406
but I respond more favorably

00:36:20.423 --> 00:36:22.848
to what the President of China

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:25.480
is saying than the President
of the United States.

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:27.160
The President of China at least says

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:31.160
that he's looking forward
to a harmonious relationship

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.670
over time and that he's
looking for the world

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:37.120
being divided up through
different spheres of influence,

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:41.220
and that China will take
care of the Asian sphere.

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:44.660
But looking for peaceable outcomes,

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:48.463
where we're all threats and demands,

00:36:50.195 --> 00:36:53.910
and, so it's a different story being told.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:55.760
Now, there's some justice, of course,

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:59.610
in what Trump is doing.

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:01.900
It is true, during our
period of leadership,

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:04.913
we tended to overlook, in our own country,

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:09.390
some of the problems that
world leadership implied

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:12.610
in terms of willing to
accept a lot of imports,

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:14.180
in particular.

00:37:14.180 --> 00:37:17.930
And we began getting
overwhelmed with imports

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:20.160
and it's only part of the sections,

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:23.690
didn't bother Wall Street,
doesn't bother California,

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:25.877
but it bothered all those
small manufacturers,

00:37:25.877 --> 00:37:29.850
and not so small manufacturers,
that were losing out.

00:37:29.850 --> 00:37:33.350
And it's true, we do have a
big current account deficit,

00:37:33.350 --> 00:37:36.640
it's not all China, and China no longer

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:39.510
has the big surplus that it had worldwide.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:42.760
So, we may not be analyzing
this all correctly,

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:45.870
but there's no doubt
we've had some unevenness

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.450
and repercussions in the United States,

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:51.870
Which was, then, a real political problem,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:53.660
And we've got to resolve it.

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:56.170
- That political problem is the polarity,

00:37:56.170 --> 00:37:58.343
that political problem is the wealth gap?

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:01.750
Describe the political problem.

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:03.670
- Well, to reach some agreement upon what

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:06.393
an appropriate influx,
under what conditions,

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:09.000
immigrants are.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.760
We've, in recent years,
taken a lot of immigrants,

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:16.440
maybe we don't want so many,
but what should the rules be?

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:18.680
Both in terms of our economic needs,

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:20.400
but in terms of our moral needs?

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:25.360
When people are being
oppressed in different ways.

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:28.430
And how can we develop an
economy that's more balanced

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:32.320
between the great middle
part of the country

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:34.530
and the two coastal areas,

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:37.750
where a lot of the vigor seems to exist?

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:38.780
And that's a big challenge,

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:40.920
it's not gonna be healed over night.

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:43.750
What are we gonna do
about the big issues of,

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:46.873
from my point of view, climate change?

00:38:48.170 --> 00:38:52.230
We don't seem to be able
to reach and work with

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:55.210
other countries effectively in that area,

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:58.020
which may kill the whole next generation

00:38:58.020 --> 00:38:59.477
if we don't worry about it.

00:38:59.477 --> 00:39:03.200
So there are a lot of
issues that, somehow,

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:06.993
have been polarized to the extent,

00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:11.510
we're having great difficulty
in achieving the consensus

00:39:11.510 --> 00:39:12.930
that we need.

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:16.425
- Paul, what would you
like your legacy to be?

00:39:16.425 --> 00:39:18.990
- Oh, God, my legacy,
I'd like to see my legacy

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:21.730
in more effective government in general.

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:24.580
I haven't got any control
over the high politics,

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:26.100
or any influence,

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:28.950
but I would like to see
a general acceptance

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:33.660
of the need for an efficient
public service ethic.

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:37.100
And see people attracted
to our public service

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:38.880
because they can take satisfaction

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:41.380
that they're doing something concrete

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:44.950
for the country and the people, generally.

00:39:44.950 --> 00:39:49.100
And it can certainly test their own acumen

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:53.403
and intelligence, and energy,
to do that kind of thing.

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:57.440
My father, I'm sure, got restive

00:39:57.440 --> 00:40:00.040
about spending so much time in government.

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:02.210
But I'm sure that, in the end,

00:40:02.210 --> 00:40:07.140
he found that a satisfying career.

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:10.810
And, didn't make a lot of money,
he made enough to live on,

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:13.150
Simply enough, I would like to see

00:40:13.150 --> 00:40:18.150
that kind of spirit permeate
in a wider population.

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:20.490
And it once did, there's
no doubt about it.

00:40:20.490 --> 00:40:23.070
When I grew up, when I got out of college

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:25.720
and I got out of graduate school,

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:28.520
going into government was
a popular thing to do.

00:40:28.520 --> 00:40:30.170
Now people scratch their head and say,

00:40:30.170 --> 00:40:32.220
why would you ever want to do that?

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.370
And that's not a very healthy sign,

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:38.690
which goes along with the 20% confidence

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:40.360
in government to do things right.

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:42.853
Back when Kennedy came
in, it was overdone.

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:46.800
With all the spirit that he
had, and I was a young man,

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:51.269
and full of Kennedy glamour,

00:40:51.269 --> 00:40:52.752
or a respect for it.

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:57.471
You asked that same question,
do you trust your government?

00:40:57.471 --> 00:41:00.890
60% or 70% said yes, now that's a healthy,

00:41:00.890 --> 00:41:04.710
we should be skeptical, I
want 30% to be skeptical.

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:07.340
I want more than 30% to be skeptical.

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:08.860
Being skeptical is one thing,

00:41:08.860 --> 00:41:11.480
but being anti-government
is another thing.

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.700
We repair it, we don't dismiss it.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:15.860
- What does the Volcker Alliance do?

00:41:15.860 --> 00:41:19.010
- We are a young and small organization.

00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:22.310
But we're trying to get some cohesiveness

00:41:22.310 --> 00:41:25.330
is what we perceive, many people perceive,

00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:29.290
about the needs of government
for effective administration

00:41:29.290 --> 00:41:31.020
and effective people.

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:33.350
And get better connection
between the needs

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:35.870
of the government and the universities,

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:39.140
with schools of public
administration or public policy

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:42.540
that can feed this potential demand

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:47.453
more effectively than
has been done recently.

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:51.390
The recruitment between
the federal government

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:55.210
and young people, and between
these universities is so lax

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:57.310
it's almost disappeared.

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:00.130
And that's not right, these universities

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:02.750
have to review what they're doing

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:05.890
to see how they can really
educate effectively.

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:08.866
And the government has
to cooperate with them

00:42:08.866 --> 00:42:10.920
and see what kind of people they need

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:13.020
and how they need to be trained.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:16.307
How they can be employed
with a minimum of red tape

00:42:16.307 --> 00:42:21.290
and delays, and be given satisfying jobs.

00:42:21.290 --> 00:42:22.460
Because there's no doubt there are a lot

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:23.590
of challenges in government,

00:42:23.590 --> 00:42:28.560
a lot of opportunities
for challenging jobs.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:31.010
- How would you hope that the government

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:33.370
would work with the Volcker Alliance

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:35.500
to visualize that goal?

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:36.774
What parts of the
government would you want--

00:42:36.774 --> 00:42:40.269
- Well, you ask a sensitive question.

00:42:40.269 --> 00:42:43.100
We're examining precisely that issue.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:45.250
There are people in the OMB,

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:47.630
Office of Management and Budget.

00:42:47.630 --> 00:42:49.890
The management part of
that has been very weak

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:50.840
but it still exists.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:54.580
It's been there, I guess,
since the, I don't know,

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:55.930
the Truman administration.

00:42:55.930 --> 00:43:00.930
That should be a central focus
for government management.

00:43:01.890 --> 00:43:05.160
With a few experts and interested people.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:07.980
They now are taking an
initiative to do precisely

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:09.370
what I'm talking about.

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:14.240
So we had seven or eight
questions, we asked all 50 states,

00:43:14.240 --> 00:43:15.830
with the help of some of the universities,

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:18.740
how are you doing in budgeting?

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:21.650
How are you doing, [do you have] funds

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:23.630
to reserve for emergencies?

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:26.670
How's your pension system?

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:28.960
And three or four other questions.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.710
We published a nice, shiny book.

00:43:32.710 --> 00:43:35.840
All the states were ranked,
we didn't rank them in order

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:38.130
but we said are you doing a good job,

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:41.110
bad job, impossible job?

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:42.530
And this attracted a lot of attention

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:45.230
because it's a competition
between the states.

00:43:45.230 --> 00:43:48.290
So people say, oh my
God, here we got a report

00:43:48.290 --> 00:43:49.650
by the Volcker Alliance that says

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:51.923
we don't have good budgeting arrangements.

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:54.370
So, some of them have actually changed

00:43:54.370 --> 00:43:57.710
their budgeting procedures.

00:43:57.710 --> 00:43:59.600
- That's a good example of the impact

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:00.770
of the Volcker Alliance.

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:03.420
By being able to put the grades on those

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:05.790
for everybody to see, it changes behavior.

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:07.810
- Everybody talks about infrastructure.

00:44:07.810 --> 00:44:09.563
Nobody's doing anything about it.

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.930
That I can see, anyway, ever.

00:44:12.930 --> 00:44:15.180
It's obvious infrastructure projects

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:18.840
is new rail tunnels under the Hudson.

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:20.850
We're sitting here with leaking tunnels,

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:24.420
totally dependent upon
these two very fragile

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:27.410
existing pieces of infrastructure

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:30.940
that we built more than 100 years ago?

00:44:30.940 --> 00:44:34.169
What are we wasting time on a few blocks

00:44:34.169 --> 00:44:36.130
at Second Avenue Subway?

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:38.550
Why are we wasting a lot of time with

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:41.640
some elaborate way of
bringing two or three

00:44:41.640 --> 00:44:44.780
Long Island trains into
Grand Central Station?

00:44:44.780 --> 00:44:49.490
When big, obvious needs are going undone.

00:44:49.490 --> 00:44:51.450
So, I'm trying to make a little project

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:55.100
to bring out some of those problems.

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:57.750
And does it take a federal
government leadership

00:44:57.750 --> 00:45:01.940
to somehow bring people
together to see which are the

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:04.990
most urgent of these
infrastructure problems?

00:45:04.990 --> 00:45:08.370
And to what extent should
the federal government,

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:11.263
that inevitably gets involved,
be doing the financing?

00:45:12.140 --> 00:45:14.230
And checking its viability,

00:45:14.230 --> 00:45:16.720
working with the states and cities.

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:18.530
And it's just not being done now

00:45:18.530 --> 00:45:20.557
in the way it should be done.

00:45:20.557 --> 00:45:24.950
And the states are all
being squeezed financially,

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:27.323
which makes it all the more difficult.

00:45:28.930 --> 00:45:33.500
- How can others help
you achieve your legacy

00:45:33.500 --> 00:45:35.620
and the Volcker Alliance to--

00:45:35.620 --> 00:45:40.620
- Well, they can look it up
on their iPhone, (laughs)

00:45:41.650 --> 00:45:42.580
I guess they can see.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:45.750
But we do need some money
and we're at a point

00:45:45.750 --> 00:45:50.750
where we've got some money to
function this year, next year,

00:45:50.850 --> 00:45:52.260
but if we're gonna continue after that,

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:56.210
we're gonna need some
influx of real money.

00:45:56.210 --> 00:45:57.950
And we're a very small organization,

00:45:57.950 --> 00:45:59.910
but it's interesting what you can do

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:02.150
with a small organization.

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:04.680
But we do have to get, and
we're gonna spend this year

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:09.420
making another try of
demanding more cooperation

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:11.310
from the universities themselves.

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:14.793
Because they, potentially, have
more resources than we have.

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:18.200
Public administration, public management,

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:20.820
they put a lot of money
into business management.

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:23.490
Not putting money in
the public management.

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:26.253
Economics, it was a prestigious,

00:46:27.644 --> 00:46:30.290
I hesitate to call it a science,

00:46:30.290 --> 00:46:33.440
but it's got great prestige, or it did,

00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:38.440
until it missed the 2008
crisis pretty completely.

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:42.000
Economists have lost a
lot of prestige, I think,

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:44.763
in recent years, and rightfully so.

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:47.670
The amount of money that's been poured

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:49.340
into economic research

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:52.440
as compared to the amount of money poured

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:56.423
into research and public
administration is astonishing.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:00.780
It just hasn't got the same respect.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.450
My old university of Princeton,

00:47:02.450 --> 00:47:07.400
once got a huge donation
for public managing,

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:09.033
training for public service.

00:47:11.030 --> 00:47:14.430
Where's the money, it goes
to the economics department

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:16.150
to oversimplify.

00:47:16.150 --> 00:47:19.730
When I went to Princeton,
the president of Princeton

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:22.183
was a professor of public administration.

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:26.620
I tell people that now, in
the economics department,

00:47:26.620 --> 00:47:27.720
they don't believe it.

00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:32.310
Woodrow Wilson was the father
of public administration.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:34.270
President of Princeton.

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:36.100
One of the things you did not touch upon,

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:38.360
which I think are a big problem,

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:41.303
is the amount of corruption in the world.

00:47:42.190 --> 00:47:46.690
Fiduciary responsibility
seems to have vanished

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:50.270
from the, doesn't vanish
from their statements,

00:47:50.270 --> 00:47:53.560
the nice statements of
ethics that they print.

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:56.300
But when it gets down to the trading desk,

00:47:56.300 --> 00:47:59.543
when it gets down to making
a deal with Malaysia,

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:02.163
with the big payoff,

00:48:05.290 --> 00:48:07.080
you wonder whether the ethical standards

00:48:07.080 --> 00:48:09.230
ever get down to the
people doing the work.

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:13.440
And, enormous number of
conflicts of interest

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:18.440
that have developed in banks,
and elsewhere in finance.

00:48:19.930 --> 00:48:23.060
- I did a study of what
made countries succeed

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:26.390
and fail with a 10 year time arise,

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:28.270
and looked at different indicators.

00:48:28.270 --> 00:48:32.770
And there's a negative 52% correlation,

00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:35.520
in other words, a very
strong negative correlation,

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:37.900
between the level of corruption

00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:40.470
and the level of relative growth.

00:48:40.470 --> 00:48:42.760
You recently received a lot of attention

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:47.160
for making the statement
that, related to government,

00:48:47.160 --> 00:48:51.010
that it's a hell of a mess all around.

00:48:51.010 --> 00:48:53.330
And I think you were talking more broadly,

00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:55.260
but clarify what you mean by that.

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:58.090
- We have fake news, you
don't know what to believe,

00:48:58.090 --> 00:48:59.110
what not to believe.

00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:02.810
You got presidents that don't seem to mind

00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:06.750
either personal behavior or
making outrageous statements,

00:49:06.750 --> 00:49:08.040
true or not.

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:11.530
You have a Congress that's been unable

00:49:11.530 --> 00:49:13.520
to function effectively.

00:49:13.520 --> 00:49:16.860
Maybe, hopefully, maybe
that'll change a little bit

00:49:16.860 --> 00:49:19.960
but we'll wait and see
after this past election.

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:23.073
But we have not been on
a constructive track.

00:49:24.491 --> 00:49:25.850
I think that's fair to say.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.630
We rammed through a massive tax bill,

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:31.680
whatever you think about that tax bill,

00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:33.200
shouldn't be rammed through the Congress

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:37.060
without any debates, at
midnight, on December 31st,

00:49:37.060 --> 00:49:38.770
or whatever was done.

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:40.380
With almost nobody in the Congress

00:49:40.380 --> 00:49:43.290
having a very good idea
of what was in the bill,

00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:45.886
and whether you like
what was in it or not,

00:49:45.886 --> 00:49:48.690
it wasn't subject to
the right kind of debate

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:49.793
that we should've had.

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:55.113
But that seems to be true
in other areas as well.

00:49:56.154 --> 00:49:59.170
I just refer you to the morning press

00:49:59.170 --> 00:50:01.720
if you wanna know a description of

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:04.980
how effective government is these days.

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:07.230
- So, a lot of people have claimed

00:50:07.230 --> 00:50:10.593
that we've always had
challenges in government,

00:50:10.593 --> 00:50:12.260
and we've been through these things.

00:50:12.260 --> 00:50:13.750
- Including my mother.

00:50:13.750 --> 00:50:16.170
Well, in my lifetime, yes.

00:50:16.170 --> 00:50:20.460
I think there was a general,
after World War II, again,

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:23.763
when we were king of the crop,

00:50:24.600 --> 00:50:28.690
there was a broad consensus
about the broad outlines

00:50:28.690 --> 00:50:30.510
of foreign policy.

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:32.980
And there was a willingness,
of both Republican

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:36.010
and Democratic presidents,

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:39.493
to lead NATO,

00:50:39.501 --> 00:50:42.226
lead the World Trade Organization,

00:50:42.630 --> 00:50:47.630
lead the whole effort
to reduce terrorists,

00:50:47.750 --> 00:50:49.820
that all went hand in hand.

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:51.280
There were a lot of debates about it

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:56.280
but the basic core of the
policy was generally accepted.

00:50:56.550 --> 00:50:59.410
And that's changed.

00:50:59.410 --> 00:51:01.980
And we're not always
gonna have happy harmony

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:03.490
about what policies should be

00:51:03.490 --> 00:51:05.560
but you've got to do a little better

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:07.430
than we've been doing recently.

00:51:07.430 --> 00:51:10.990
I go back to, what I think
I said some time ago,

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:13.970
of all the presidential appointments,

00:51:13.970 --> 00:51:16.820
far less than half anybody's
even been nominated,

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:18.830
much less appointed.

00:51:18.830 --> 00:51:23.310
The quality of the people
appointed to cabinet offices,

00:51:23.310 --> 00:51:24.653
and sub cabinet offices,

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:29.540
I think by any estimation
is not up to scratch.

00:51:29.540 --> 00:51:31.460
That didn't say they were not good

00:51:31.460 --> 00:51:34.200
and effective appointments,

00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:37.316
but when you see lobbyists

00:51:37.316 --> 00:51:40.116
for polluting companies

00:51:40.116 --> 00:51:45.090
named head of the agency
for anti-pollution,

00:51:45.270 --> 00:51:50.270
you wonder about the coherent
nature of the appointment.

00:51:53.470 --> 00:51:55.745
And it's happening too often.

00:51:55.745 --> 00:51:57.250
The state department's ripped apart,

00:51:57.250 --> 00:51:59.460
maybe it should be ripped apart.

00:51:59.460 --> 00:52:01.250
But it ought to be done
in a constructive way.

00:52:01.250 --> 00:52:04.670
We haven't had an
ambassador in Saudi Arabia.

00:52:04.670 --> 00:52:06.410
We have all these problems
with Saudi Arabia,

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:09.840
we haven't even got an
ambassador there until now,

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:11.100
why not?

00:52:11.100 --> 00:52:14.260
Too much policy seems to be
out of the back pocket today,

00:52:14.260 --> 00:52:17.130
and God knows what happens tomorrow.

00:52:17.130 --> 00:52:19.040
Look, for an old government man like me,

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:22.310
it doesn't look very
good, hence my comment.

00:52:22.310 --> 00:52:24.390
Let's hope it gets better.

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:27.280
- Well, thank you, Paul,
for spending this time

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:30.500
and also conveying some
of your principles,

00:52:30.500 --> 00:52:34.440
and also some of what you're
hoping will be achieved

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:37.453
over the next few years,
to make up your legacy.

00:52:38.910 --> 00:52:41.180
I hope that we can help you achieve that.

00:52:41.180 --> 00:52:42.600
- Well, thank you for coming,

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:44.916
I appreciate the opportunity

00:52:44.916 --> 00:52:48.957
to rant and rave a little bit

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:50.810
because I think the country needs a little

00:52:50.810 --> 00:52:52.230
ranting and raving.

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:53.493
- And you do that so well.

00:52:53.493 --> 00:52:55.347
- (laughing) I don't know about that.

